<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhaskara II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1114–1185</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Bhaskaracharya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhaskara II was an Indian mathematician and astronomer, author of the Siddhanta Shiromani, whose parts include the Lilavati on arithmetic and the Bijaganita on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>. His work marks a high point of classical Indian mathematics. In 1207 his grandson Changadeva founded a school at Patan to teach his works, under the patronage of the Seuna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> king Singhana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhaskara-ii.jpg" alt="Bhaskara II" width="300" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%A4%AD%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%95%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9A%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B0%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
